## Business Planning
@ Executive summary | Market analysis | Operations plan | Financial plan
Which section gives decision-makers the proposal's essentials?|0|It condenses the business case.
Which section examines customers, demand and competition?|1|Market evidence tests commercial opportunity.
Which section explains production, staffing and delivery?|2|Operations describe how work is done.
Which section projects costs, funding and financial outcomes?|3|Financial planning quantifies resource needs.
@ Target customer | Value proposition | Revenue model | Cost structure
Who is the intended buyer?|0|The target defines whom the business serves.
What explains why customers would choose the offering?|1|The proposition states a meaningful benefit.
What describes how the business earns income?|2|Revenue mechanisms specify payment flows.
What describes major expenses and their behavior?|3|Cost structure organizes expenditure.
@ Strength | Weakness | Opportunity | Threat
An existing skilled team is classified how in SWOT?|0|It is an internal advantage.
A current lack of essential equipment is classified how?|1|It is an internal limitation.
An underserved emerging market is classified how?|2|It is an external possibility.
A new competitor likely to reduce demand is classified how?|3|It is an external risk.
@ Feasibility test | Milestone | Contingency plan | Performance indicator
Checking whether resources and demand can support a proposal is what?|0|Feasibility tests workable assumptions.
A dated major deliverable is what?|1|Milestones track key progress.
A prepared response to supply interruption is what?|2|Contingencies anticipate disruption.
A measurable outcome used to assess progress is what?|3|Indicators connect plans with results.
@ Test assumptions | Match forecasts to evidence | Separate profit from cash availability | Revise the plan transparently
A plan assumes every survey respondent will buy. What should be done?|0|Interest does not establish purchase behavior.
Sales estimates exceed the reachable market. What needs correction?|1|Forecasts need a defensible basis.
Profits are projected but supplier bills fall due before receipts. What must be analyzed?|2|Timing affects liquidity.
A supplier withdraws before launch. What is appropriate?|3|New facts can require revised operations and financing.
## Marketing & the Marketing Mix
@ Product | Price | Place | Promotion
Features and benefits of an offering belong to which P?|0|Product concerns the offering itself.
The amount customers pay belongs to which P?|1|Price concerns exchange value.
Distribution channels belong to which P?|2|Place concerns availability and delivery.
Communication intended to inform or persuade belongs to which P?|3|Promotion communicates with the market.
@ Segmentation | Targeting | Positioning | Market research
Dividing a market into meaningful groups is what?|0|Segments have relevant shared characteristics.
Selecting the groups to serve is what?|1|Targeting chooses intended customers.
Defining a distinctive place in customers' minds is what?|2|Positioning concerns perceived value relative to alternatives.
Gathering evidence about buyers and competitors is what?|3|Research informs marketing decisions.
@ Need | Want | Effective demand | Customer value
A basic requirement such as nourishment illustrates what?|0|Needs concern fundamental requirements.
Preference for a particular brand or format illustrates what?|1|Wants specify a preferred satisfaction.
Willingness combined with ability to buy illustrates what?|2|Demand requires purchasing capability.
Perceived benefits relative to costs illustrate what?|3|Value is judged through benefits and sacrifices.
@ Distribution fit | Message clarity | Price feasibility | Product suitability
Customers cannot reach the only outlet. Which issue needs review?|0|Channel access affects availability.
An advertisement leaves the actual offer unclear. Which issue?|1|Communication must be understandable.
The selling price is below sustainable total cost. Which issue?|2|Pricing must consider viability.
The offering fails to address the intended problem. Which issue?|3|Features must serve the target need.
@ Correlation does not isolate campaign effects | Truthful claims require evidence | One segment does not represent all buyers | The marketing mix should work together
Sales rise during both advertising and a holiday. What caution applies?|0|Seasonal changes may also contribute.
A promotion promises a health benefit without support. What is required?|1|A marketing purpose does not establish factual truth.
Only current loyal customers were surveyed about wider demand. What limitation matters?|2|Prospective buyers may differ.
A premium positioning uses unreliable delivery and poor support. What needs review?|3|The promised value must align with the actual offering.
## Fundamentals of Accountancy
@ Asset | Liability | Owner's equity | Revenue
A resource controlled by a business is classified as what?|0|Assets provide expected economic benefits.
An obligation owed to outsiders is what?|1|Liabilities represent obligations.
The residual interest after liabilities is what?|2|Equity equals assets less liabilities.
Income earned from ordinary business activities is what?|3|Revenue arises from operations.
@ Debit | Credit | Journal | Ledger
Which side is the left side of an account?|0|The debit side is left.
Which side is the right side of an account?|1|The credit side is right.
Where are transactions initially recorded chronologically?|2|A journal records entries by date.
Where are entries organized by individual account?|3|A ledger groups account activity.
@ ₱600 | ₱800 | ₱1000 | ₱1200
Assets are ₱1000 and liabilities ₱400. Equity?|0|1000 minus 400 equals 600.
Assets are ₱1200 and equity ₱400. Liabilities?|1|1200 minus 400 equals 800.
Liabilities are ₱300 and equity ₱700. Assets?|2|300 plus 700 equals 1000.
Equity is ₱900 and liabilities ₱300. Assets?|3|900 plus 300 equals 1200.
@ Accrual accounting | Going concern | Business entity | Historical cost
Recognizing earned revenue rather than merely cash receipt illustrates what?|0|Accrual timing follows economic activity.
Preparing accounts on an expectation of continued operation uses what assumption?|1|Going concern assumes continuing business activity.
Keeping the owner's personal spending separate from business records uses what concept?|2|The entity is accounted for separately.
Recording an acquired asset initially at its transaction cost uses what basis?|3|Historical cost is based on acquisition.
@ Every entry must balance | Cash receipt is not always revenue | Owner investment is not operating income | Profit is not identical to cash flow
Why must total debits equal total credits in an entry?|0|Double entry preserves the accounting equation.
A loan deposits cash in the bank. Why is it not sales revenue?|1|It creates a repayment obligation.
The owner contributes capital. How should its nature be understood?|2|It is a financing contribution, not earned revenue.
A profitable business cannot pay a bill today. What distinction matters?|3|Accrual earnings and cash timing can differ.
## Accounting for Service & Merchandising
@ Service business | Merchandising business | Inventory | Cost of goods sold
A firm earns fees by performing work for clients. What type?|0|Its primary output is a service.
A store buys goods for resale. What type?|1|Merchandising involves resale.
Goods held for sale are recorded as what?|2|Inventory is an asset before sale.
The assigned cost of goods already sold is what?|3|It becomes an expense of sales.
@ Sales revenue | Gross profit | Operating expense | Net income
The selling amount earned from merchandise is what?|0|Sales revenue records earned sales.
Net sales minus cost of goods sold equals what?|1|Gross profit precedes other operating expenses.
Rent used in store operations is normally what?|2|It supports running the business.
Revenues less all relevant expenses determine what?|3|Net income is the period's profit.
@ Perpetual inventory | Periodic inventory | Sales return | Purchase return
Which inventory system updates inventory continuously with transactions?|0|Perpetual records update stock as activity occurs.
Which system determines cost of goods sold using period-end inventory calculations?|1|Periodic accounting uses the ending count.
A customer returns merchandise to the seller. What transaction?|2|It reverses part of the sale as appropriate.
A retailer sends goods back to its supplier. What transaction?|3|It reverses part of the purchase.
@ ₱200 | ₱300 | ₱400 | ₱500
Net sales ₱800 less cost of goods sold ₱600 gives gross profit of what?|0|800 minus 600 equals 200.
Net sales ₱1000 less cost of goods sold ₱700 gives what gross profit?|1|1000 minus 700 equals 300.
Opening stock ₱200 plus purchases ₱500 less ending stock ₱300 gives cost of goods sold of what?|2|Goods available less ending stock equals 400.
Gross profit ₱700 less operating expenses ₱200 gives net income of what, with no other items?|3|700 minus 200 equals 500.
@ Record both sale and inventory cost | Distinguish unsold inventory from expense | Check stock records against physical counts | Assess margins, not sales alone
Under perpetual inventory, why is recording sales revenue alone incomplete?|0|Inventory and cost of goods sold also change.
Why should all purchased merchandise not immediately become cost of goods sold?|1|Unsold merchandise remains an asset.
A count differs from inventory records. What should be investigated?|2|Shrinkage or recording errors may explain differences.
Sales increase but discounts and costs increase faster. What is needed before claiming improved profit?|3|Higher sales need not improve earnings.
## Business Mathematics
@ Ratio | Proportion | Unit price | Percentage
Which comparison expresses one quantity relative to another?|0|A ratio relates quantities.
Which statement asserts equality of two ratios?|1|A proportion equates ratios.
Cost per single standard quantity is what?|2|Unit pricing permits comparison.
A fraction expressed per hundred is what?|3|Percent means per hundred.
@ ₱20 | ₱25 | ₱30 | ₱40
Five items cost ₱100. Unit cost?|0|100 divided by 5 equals 20.
Four items cost ₱100. Unit cost?|1|100 divided by 4 equals 25.
Three items cost ₱90. Unit cost?|2|90 divided by 3 equals 30.
Two items cost ₱80. Unit cost?|3|80 divided by 2 equals 40.
@ 10 | 15 | 20 | 25
A 2:3 ratio has first quantity 10. Second quantity?|1|Multiply both ratio parts by five.
A recipe needs 2 cups per batch. Cups for 10 batches?|2|2 multiplied by 10 equals 20.
Twenty percent of 50 equals what?|0|0.20 multiplied by 50 equals 10.
One quarter of 100 equals what?|3|100 divided by 4 equals 25.
@ ₱120 | ₱150 | ₱180 | ₱200
A ₱100 cost receives 20% markup on cost. Selling price?|0|100 plus 20 equals 120.
A ₱200 price is reduced by 25%. Sale price?|1|200 multiplied by 0.75 equals 150.
A ₱150 amount rises by 20%. New amount?|2|150 multiplied by 1.2 equals 180.
Revenue is ₱500 and total cost ₱300. Profit?|3|500 minus 300 equals 200.
@ Compare common units | Keep bases explicit | Round at the appropriate step | Check reasonableness
Different pack sizes have different prices. What enables a fair comparison?|0|Quantities need a common basis.
Why distinguish markup on cost from margin on sales?|1|The denominators differ.
Why can rounding every intermediate calculation distort a final bill?|2|Small rounding errors can accumulate.
A routine unit-price calculation gives a result larger than the whole package price. What should be done?|3|The result may indicate a unit or operation error.
## Markup, Markdown & Payroll
@ Markup | Markdown | Gross pay | Net pay
An amount added to cost to set a selling price is what?|0|Markup raises price above the chosen cost base.
A reduction from an existing selling price is what?|1|Markdown lowers that price.
Pay before deductions is what?|2|Gross pay precedes withholdings.
Pay after applicable deductions is what?|3|Net pay is the take-home amount.
@ ₱10 | ₱20 | ₱25 | ₱50
A 10% markup on cost ₱100 adds what?|0|100 multiplied by 0.10 equals 10.
A 20% markdown on price ₱100 subtracts what?|1|100 multiplied by 0.20 equals 20.
A 25% markdown on price ₱100 subtracts what?|2|100 multiplied by 0.25 equals 25.
A 50% markup on cost ₱100 adds what?|3|100 multiplied by 0.50 equals 50.
@ ₱400 | ₱450 | ₱500 | ₱550
A worker earns ₱100 per hour for four ordinary hours under a stated example contract. Gross pay?|0|100 multiplied by 4 equals 400.
Example gross pay ₱500 less stated deductions ₱50 gives net pay of what?|1|500 minus 50 equals 450.
A product costs ₱400 with markup ₱100. Selling price?|2|400 plus 100 equals 500.
A product priced ₱600 has a ₱50 markdown. Sale price?|3|600 minus 50 equals 550.
@ Time record | Pay rate | Deduction basis | Payslip
Which record establishes hours credited for pay?|0|Recorded time supports the calculation.
Which agreed amount is multiplied by payable time or units?|1|The rate determines earnings per unit.
What must support each withheld amount?|2|Deductions require an applicable legitimate basis.
Which document itemizes earnings and deductions?|3|A payslip explains the pay calculation.
@ Distinguish cost and sales bases | Sequential reductions multiply | Verify current applicable rules | Separate gross from net
An item costs ₱80 and sells for ₱100. Why are 25% markup and 20% margin both possible?|0|The same ₱20 uses different denominators.
Why are two successive 10% markdowns not a single 20% markdown?|1|0.9 multiplied by 0.9 equals 0.81.
Why should a sample classroom deduction rate not be treated as actual law?|2|Real payroll obligations depend on current rules and circumstances.
Why cannot a change in take-home pay alone identify a change in salary rate?|3|Hours and deductions may also change.
## Business Finance
@ Investment decision | Financing decision | Working capital management | Risk management
Selecting long-term business assets concerns what?|0|Investment allocates funds to productive uses.
Choosing debt or owner funding concerns what?|1|Financing determines sources of capital.
Managing cash, receivables and short-term obligations concerns what?|2|Working capital supports daily liquidity.
Assessing exposure to uncertain losses concerns what?|3|Risk management examines uncertainty and response.
@ Liquidity | Profitability | Solvency | Return
Ability to meet near-term payment needs concerns what?|0|Liquidity concerns accessible funds.
Ability to generate earnings relative to activity concerns what?|1|Profitability evaluates earnings performance.
Ability to sustain obligations over the longer term concerns what?|2|Solvency concerns longer-term financial capacity.
Gain or loss relative to an investment concerns what?|3|Return relates outcome to invested resources.
@ Debt | Equity | Interest | Dividend
Borrowed funds with repayment obligations are what?|0|Debt creates creditor claims.
Owner contributions and residual claims are what?|1|Equity represents ownership interest.
The cost charged for borrowed money is what?|2|Interest compensates lending under agreed terms.
A distribution of corporate earnings to shareholders is what?|3|A dividend distributes value to owners.
@ Cash budget | Receivables | Payables | Financial forecast
Which plan estimates cash receipts and payments by period?|0|A cash budget tracks timing.
Amounts customers owe are what?|1|Receivables are claims to payment.
Amounts owed to suppliers are what?|2|Payables are obligations.
A reasoned estimate of future financial results is what?|3|A forecast depends on assumptions.
@ Higher expected return may involve greater risk | Profit and liquidity are distinct | Diversification does not remove every risk | Financing must fit repayment capacity
Why should an unusually high promised return be examined carefully?|0|Its risk or credibility may be problematic.
Why can credit sales increase earnings without immediately supplying cash?|1|Collection may occur later.
Why does holding several investments not guarantee no loss?|2|Some risks affect many assets together.
Why can short-term borrowing for a long-lived project create difficulty?|3|Payments may fall due before the project generates cash.
## Financial Statements
@ Income statement | Statement of financial position | Statement of cash flows | Statement of changes in equity
Which statement reports revenues and expenses over a period?|0|It measures performance across time.
Which statement reports assets, liabilities and equity at a date?|1|It presents a financial position snapshot.
Which statement explains cash inflows and outflows?|2|It reports changes in cash.
Which statement explains changes in owners' residual interest?|3|It reconciles equity movements.
@ Operating activity | Investing activity | Financing activity | Noncash transaction
Cash collected from ordinary customers is generally which activity?|0|It arises from core operations.
Cash paid to acquire long-term equipment is generally which activity?|1|It invests in a long-term asset.
Cash received from issuing owner shares is which activity?|2|It is an equity financing inflow.
Equipment acquired directly through a loan without cash transfer illustrates what?|3|The exchange affects accounts without current cash movement.
@ Asset | Liability | Equity | Expense
Cash held by the entity is what account category?|0|It is a controlled resource.
A bank loan payable is what category?|1|It is an obligation.
The residual after deducting liabilities is what category?|2|It belongs to owners.
Rent consumed during the period is what category?|3|It is a cost of the period.
@ ₱100 | ₱200 | ₱300 | ₱400
Revenue ₱500 less expenses ₱400 gives net income of what?|0|Subtract expenses from revenue.
Assets ₱600 less liabilities ₱400 gives equity of what?|1|Residual equity is 200.
Opening cash ₱100 plus receipts ₱500 less payments ₱300 gives closing cash of what?|2|100 plus 500 minus 300 equals 300.
Opening equity ₱300 plus income ₱200 less drawings ₱100 gives closing equity of what?|3|300 plus 200 minus 100 equals 400.
@ Read statements together | Check period comparability | Examine accounting policies | Distinguish position from performance
Why is one favorable income figure insufficient to assess overall financial health?|0|Cash and obligations also matter.
Two reports cover different numbers of months. What limits comparison?|1|Their periods are not equivalent.
Why may similar reported totals reflect different measurement approaches?|2|Policies influence recognition and measurement.
Why cannot a year-end asset balance be interpreted as the year's profit?|3|A stock at a date differs from a period's flow.
## Capital Budgeting
@ Net present value | Internal rate of return | Payback period | Profitability index
Present value of inflows less outflows is what?|0|NPV measures discounted net value.
The discount rate making NPV zero is what?|1|IRR equates discounted inflows and outflows.
Time needed to recover an initial outlay under the stated cash-flow method is what?|2|Payback measures recovery time.
Present value of inflows divided by initial outlay is what?|3|The index expresses discounted benefit per invested unit.
@ Accept under the NPV criterion | Reject under the NPV criterion | Indifferent under the NPV criterion | Recalculate with an appropriate rate
For an independent project with positive NPV and no resource constraint, what follows?|0|It adds value under the stated assumptions.
An independent project has negative NPV at the appropriate discount rate. What does the NPV criterion prescribe?|1|It fails to earn the required return.
An independent project has exactly zero NPV and no resource constraint. What does the NPV criterion imply?|2|It just earns the required return.
A project was discounted using a rate unrelated to its risk. What should happen?|3|A suitable required return matters.
@ ₱1000 | ₱100 | 4 years | 1.1
At a 10% annual discount rate, what principal grows to ₱1210 in two years?|0|1210 divided by (1.10)² equals 1000.
The present value of inflows is ₱1000 and initial outlay ₱900. NPV?|1|1000 minus 900 equals 100.
A ₱1000 outlay returns ₱250 annually. Simple payback?|2|1000 divided by 250 equals 4.
PV of inflows is ₱1100 and outlay ₱1000. Profitability index?|3|1100 divided by 1000 equals 1.1.
@ Relevant future cash flow | Sunk cost | Opportunity cost | Sensitivity analysis
A future incremental receipt caused by the project is what?|0|It changes because the decision changes.
An irrecoverable expense already paid is what?|1|It cannot be changed by the current choice.
The value of the best forgone use of a resource is what?|2|The alternative has an economic sacrifice.
Changing assumptions to examine NPV effects is what?|3|Sensitivity analysis tests dependence on inputs.
@ Discounting reflects time value | Simple payback can omit later value | Rankings can conflict | Forecast uncertainty matters
Why is cash received years later not automatically equivalent to cash now?|0|Time, opportunity and risk affect value.
Why can choosing the shortest payback reject a more valuable long-term project?|1|Payback may ignore post-recovery cash flows.
Why should mutually exclusive projects not be selected using one percentage blindly?|2|Scale and timing can make criteria disagree.
Why should a precise spreadsheet NPV not be treated as a guaranteed outcome?|3|Its cash-flow and rate assumptions are uncertain.
## Simple & Compound Interest
@ Principal | Interest rate | Time | Maturity value
The initial invested or borrowed amount is what?|0|Principal is the starting base.
The charge or return per unit principal per period is what?|1|The rate specifies interest relative to time.
The investment duration is what?|2|Time counts the relevant periods.
Principal plus accumulated interest at the end is what?|3|Maturity value includes growth.
@ ₱50 | ₱100 | ₱150 | ₱200
Simple interest on ₱1000 at 5% for one year?|0|I = Prt = 50.
Simple interest on ₱1000 at 5% for two years?|1|1000 × 0.05 × 2 = 100.
Simple interest on ₱1000 at 5% for three years?|2|1000 × 0.05 × 3 = 150.
Simple interest on ₱1000 at 10% for two years?|3|1000 × 0.10 × 2 = 200.
@ ₱1100 | ₱1210 | ₱1331 | ₱1000
₱1000 earns 10% compounded annually. Value after one year?|0|Multiply by 1.1.
₱1000 is invested at 10% compounded annually for two years. What is its future value?|1|1000 × 1.1² = 1210.
Calculate the maturity value of ₱1000 after three annual compounding periods at 10%.|2|1000 × 1.1³ = 1331.
What is the present value of ₱1100 due in one year at 10%?|3|Discount by dividing by 1.1.
@ 0.5 year | 2 periods | 2.5% | 4 periods
Convert six months into years for an annual simple-interest formula.|0|Six of twelve months is one half.
How many semiannual compounding periods occur in one year?|1|Each period is six months.
A nominal annual rate of 10% compounded quarterly gives what rate per quarter?|2|Divide 10% by four.
How many quarterly periods occur in one year?|3|There are four quarters.
@ Match rate and time units | Compound interest earns on prior interest | Effective rate can exceed nominal rate | Compare complete contract terms
Why cannot an annual rate be multiplied by an unconverted count of months?|0|Rate and duration units must correspond.
Why does annual compounding produce ₱1210 rather than ₱1200 after two years at 10% on ₱1000?|1|Second-year interest includes first-year interest.
Why can more frequent compounding increase a positive rate's annual effective return?|2|Interest is credited and itself earns sooner.
Why may the same advertised rate not imply identical borrowing cost?|3|Fees, timing and compounding can differ.
